This instance paired sparse edge-stripe/isolated-marker tests with multiple demo geometries: periodic row/column stripes, sparse marker patterns, and local marker-to-boundary fills. The examples show why palette similarity and apparent periodicity are insufficient for transferring a fill rule across geometry. The demo with the isolated marker and long color-9 background had a highly local, color-specific transformation: a long vertical background run was painted in marker color, while a separate marker's run was changed to a lead color and then marker-color cells, with boundary rows preserved. This does not support globally coloring test rows or columns based merely on sparse markers plus colored borders.

